        "id": "window-functions",
        "title": "7. Rank rows and compute running totals",
        "takeaway": "A window calculation retains detail rows while looking across related rows.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the idea",
            "paragraphs": [
              "GROUP BY collapses a group to fewer rows. A window function uses OVER to calculate across a related set while keeping each original row. PARTITION BY starts a separate window per customer; ORDER BY defines sequence within it.",
              "ROW_NUMBER assigns sequential numbers. To select the latest order reliably, order by OrderDate descending and then OrderId descending. The identifier resolves same-date ties. RANK and DENSE_RANK treat ties differently, so select a function to match the business question rather than because its name sounds right.",
              "For a running sum, explicitly use ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW. That means every preceding row plus this one in the specified order. The outer query still needs ORDER BY to control display order."
            ],
            "example": ""
          },
          {
            "title": "Walk through an example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Ben’s two orders occur on January 2 and 3, so his running total is 80 then 200. Ada’s is 100 then 150; Chen’s is 40. The result still has five order rows."
            ],
            "example": "SELECT CustomerId, OrderId, Amount,\n SUM(Amount) OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerId ORDER BY OrderDate,OrderId\n ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS RunningTotal\nFROM #LNOrders ORDER BY CustomerId,OrderDate,OrderId;"
          },
          {
            "title": "Common mistake and better habit",
            "paragraphs": [
              "ROW_NUMBER is not a stored primary key. It can change when the input changes. For latest-row selection, rank in a CTE and filter rn=1 outside it; the alias cannot be filtered in WHERE in that same SELECT."
            ],
            "example": ""
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Return the latest order for each customer who has orders.",
          "starter": ";WITH Ranked AS (SELECT *,ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerId ORDER BY /* latest */) AS rn FROM #LNOrders)\nSELECT CustomerId,OrderId FROM Ranked WHERE rn=1 ORDER BY CustomerId;",
          "solution": ";WITH Ranked AS (SELECT CustomerId,OrderId,ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerId ORDER BY OrderDate DESC,OrderId DESC) AS rn FROM #LNOrders)\nSELECT CustomerId,OrderId FROM Ranked WHERE rn=1 ORDER BY CustomerId;",
          "checks": [
            "1/102,2/104,3/105.",
            "Dia is absent because she has no order; include her only through a separate population join."
          ]
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Why add OrderId after OrderDate to the ranking order?",
          "options": [
            "It forces an index seek",
            "It breaks same-date ties deterministically",
            "It groups all customers together"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "A unique tie-breaker prevents equal dates from leaving the selected latest row ambiguous."
        }
